---
import { linkInstagramMentions } from "../../utils/instagramMentions";
import ArtworkMeta from "../../components/art/ArtworkMeta.astro";

const metaBy = linkInstagramMentions("@sata_info, as a programme partner of 10AFEST, with @stack_xyz");
---

<div class="mt-4 max-w-3xl">
  <ArtworkMeta fields={[
    { label: "Year", value: "2026" },
    { label: "Country", value: "Hong Kong" },
    { label: "Date", value: "8 October 2026" },
    { label: "Venue", value: "Eaton HK, Hong Kong" },
    { label: "For", value: "10AFEST" },
    { label: "Presented by", value: metaBy, html: true },
  ]} />

  <p class="mb-4">
    This is a live audiovisual piece played on an ordinary web page. The performer plays the page like an
    instrument: moving the mouse, typing and scrolling make the sound. The sound then hits the page back. It
    shakes, tears and glitches the page, so doing a normal task gets harder and harder.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    As the show goes on, the hidden structure under the website starts to show through. In the end, the website
    turns on its user and strikes back.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker art-kicker mt-8 mb-4">
    Fast, beautiful, always there
  </h2>

  <p class="mb-4">
    We expect the internet to be fast now. A page that takes more than a few seconds feels broken, and most of us
    just leave.<sup>1</sup>
  </p>

  <aside class="art-margin-note" id="availability-note">
    <p style="font-family: var(--f-data); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
      <strong>Availability</strong>: how much of the time a service answers. It is counted in nines.
      “Five nines”, 99.999%, allows about five minutes of downtime in a year. The usual way to get
      there is to run the same thing in several places at once, so most of the machines are there to wait.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Corporations and engineers came up with many ideas to solve this. But fast was not enough. The page also had to
    be beautiful, and it had to be there all the time. So the interface moved from something basic and quick to
    something much more complex: frameworks, trackers, fonts, video and animation, all loaded before you can read
    one line.<sup>2</sup>
  </p>

  <aside class="art-margin-note" id="cdn-note" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
    <p style="font-family: var(--f-data); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
      <strong>CDN</strong>: content delivery network. Copies of a website are kept on many servers around the
      world, and you are answered by the one nearest to you. The page feels close because a copy of it really
      is close.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class="mb-4">
    To keep that heavy page feeling fast, the infrastructure under it has to grow as well, and so does the
    investment. <a href="#availability-note" class="art-callout">Availability</a> means many data centers, so
    that when one fails another one answers. Speed means a <a href="#cdn-note" class="art-callout">CDN</a>, and
    a CDN means local servers, placed in your own city. Every click on an ordinary page touches a building
    somewhere, with its own power and cooling.<sup>3</sup>
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    None of this is visible. A good interface is one where you never think about what is under it. Ordering a
    pizza should be the easiest thing the internet does. In this piece I let the weight come back up, until the
    page can no longer pretend to be light.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker art-kicker mt-8 mb-4">
    Side read
  </h2>

  <p class="mb-4">
    <strong>Permacomputing</strong> takes its name from permaculture. Viznut coined the term in 2020, coming from
    the demoscene, where people get the most out of very small machines.<sup>4</sup> It asks what computing looks
    like if we maintain what already exists instead of buying more: keep it small, care for the chips, expose
    the seams. Low-tech Magazine runs its website on a solar panel in Barcelona, and when the weather is bad
    for long enough, the site goes offline.<sup>5</sup>
  </p>

  <aside class="art-margin-note" id="jevons-note">
    <p style="font-family: var(--f-data); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
      <strong>Jevons paradox</strong>: when a resource gets more efficient to use, we tend to use more of it
      in total, not less. First described for coal by William Stanley Jevons in <em>The Coal Question</em> (1865).
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The <strong>green cloud</strong> movement comes from the other direction: keep the cloud, but run it on
    renewable energy and measure what it emits. The Green Web Foundation keeps an open directory of hosts that
    run on green energy, and the W3C is drafting sustainability guidelines for the web.<sup>6</sup> The big
    providers all promise carbon-free or net-zero. A lot of that depends on accounting. One analysis estimated
    that the real emissions of the in-house data centers of Google, Microsoft, Meta and Apple could be several
    times higher than reported, because bought renewable certificates are subtracted from the
    total.<sup>7</sup> And a more efficient cloud is also a cloud we
    <a href="#jevons-note" class="art-callout">use more</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    One asks us to want less. The other promises we can keep wanting the same, and only change where the power
    comes from.
  </p>

  <div class="art-footnotes">
    <p><sup>1</sup>Google / DoubleClick, <a href="https://blog.google/products/admanager/the-need-for-mobile-speed/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">“The need for mobile speed”</a> (2016): 53% of mobile site visits are likely to be abandoned if a page takes longer than 3 seconds to load.</p>
    <p><sup>2</sup>HTTP Archive, <a href="https://almanac.httparchive.org/en/2024/page-weight" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Web Almanac 2024, “Page Weight”</a>. The median desktop page loads 613 KB of JavaScript alone, 14% more than two years before.</p>
    <p><sup>3</sup>International Energy Agency, <a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai/executive-summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Energy and AI</em></a> (2025). Data centers used around 415 TWh in 2024, about 1.5% of the world's electricity, and are projected to reach around 945 TWh by 2030.</p>
    <p><sup>4</sup>Ville-Matias Heikkilä (Viznut), <a href="http://viznut.fi/texts-en/permacomputing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">“Permacomputing”</a> (2020). The principles are kept and revised by the community at <a href="https://permacomputing.net/principles/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">permacomputing.net</a>.</p>
    <p><sup>5</sup>Low-tech Magazine, <a href="https://solar.lowtechmagazine.com/about/the-solar-website/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">“About the Solar Powered Website”</a>.</p>
    <p><sup>6</sup><a href="https://www.thegreenwebfoundation.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Green Web Foundation</a>; W3C, <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/web-sustainability-guidelines/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Web Sustainability Guidelines (WSG)</a>.</p>
    <p><sup>7</sup>Isabel O'Brien, <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/sep/15/data-center-gas-emissions-tech" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">“Data center emissions probably 662% higher than big tech claims. Can it keep up the ruse?”</a>, The Guardian, 15 September 2024. The figure is the Guardian's own estimate for 2020–22, comparing location-based emissions with the reported market-based ones.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="text-sm mt-6">
    <a href="https://www.instagram.com/p/DeKF7Ppku7c/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">↗ Instagram</a>
  </p>
</div>
